    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d607">

                <pb facs="0839" n="825" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5151/979a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-03T23:00:00-04:00">January 3, 1944—11
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>15. From the Secretary of the Treasury<anchor n="11" xml:id="d607-f1"/> for
                        Adler.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d607-f2"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>A request “that, if agreeable to the U. S. Government, the Treasury’s
                        General Licenses such as No. 75, be amended appropriately” was made by Dr.
                            Kung<anchor n="13" xml:id="d607-f3"/> in a telegram addressed to
                        Secretary Morgenthau, dated November 25, 1943,<anchor n="14"
                            xml:id="d607-f4"/> which was left with Mr. Morgenthau on November 30 by
                        Ambassador Wei, regarding termination of 1941 agreement.<anchor n="15"
                            xml:id="d607-f5"/>
                    </item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The changes which the dissolution of the Stabilization Board will make
                        necessary in Foreign Funds Control documents are being given consideration
                        by the Treasury, which suggests that: <list>
                            <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                            <item>The Central Bank of China, or the Commission for Control of
                                Foreign Exchange Assets, or other appropriate Chinese institution
                                assume the functions of the Stabilization Board under General
                                Licenses Nos. 58 and 75 and that Foreign Funds documents be amended
                                accordingly;</item>
                            <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)</label>
                            <item>Treasury will now consult with the appropriate Chinese institution
                                through Treasury representative in China in those cases wherein
                                Treasury would have consulted with the Stabilization Board before
                                granting licenses;</item>
                            <pb facs="0840" n="826" xml:id="pg826"/>
                            <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>)</label>
                            <item>Treasury, in its licensing policy here, would follow a more
                                liberal policy in cases where private Americans have made
                                arrangements for relief, charitable, and religious organizations,
                                news correspondents, and other individuals or groups to overcome
                                difficulties arising out of exchange rate.</item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <label>(3)</label>
                    <item>The Treasury would be pleased to receive your views and comments on above
                        proposals, including answer to the question whether a stiff attitude against
                        approving transactions which involve Americans making special arrangements
                        to overcome official exchange rate is likely to be taken by appropriate
                        Chinese institution in case it is consulted by the Treasury as the
                        Stabilization Board was. [Morgenthau.]</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d607-f1" n="11" xml:id="d607-f1-content">
                <p>Henry Morgenthau, Jr.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d607-f2" n="12" xml:id="d607-f2-content">
                <p>Solomon Adler, alternate American member of the Chinese Stabilization Board; upon
                    liquidation of the Stabilization Board Mr. Adler remained in China as Treasury
                    Department representative.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d607-f3" n="13" xml:id="d607-f3-content">
                <p>H. H. Kung, Chinese Minister of Finance.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d607-f4" n="14" xml:id="d607-f4-content">
                <p>Not found in Department files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d607-f5" n="15" xml:id="d607-f5-content">
                <p>For previous correspondence on this subject, see <ref
                        target="frus1943China#pg_457">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1943, China, pp. 457</ref>
                    ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
